{
  "dom_id": "step-q74864",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=i,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j}{1} \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot (-j) \\\\  & =(i)\\cdot (-j) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
